      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Given that \\( x - \\frac{1}{x} = 4 \\), we start by finding \\( x^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} \\).\n\nFirst, we square both sides of the given equation:\n\\[\n\\left( x - \\frac{1}{x} \\right)^2 = 4^2\n\\]\nThis expands to:\n\\[\nx^2 - 2 \\cdot x \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} + \\frac{1}{x^2} = 16\n\\]\nSimplifying the middle term, we get:\n\\[\nx^2 - 2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} = 16\n\\]\nAdding 2 to both sides, we obtain:\n\\[\nx^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} = 18\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find \\( x^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} \\). We use the identity:\n\\[\nx^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} = \\left( x - \\frac{1}{x} \\right) \\left( x^2 + 1 + \\frac{1}{x^2} \\right)\n\\]\n\nSubstitute the known values \\( x - \\frac{1}{x} = 4 \\) and \\( x^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} = 18 \\):\n\\[\nx^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} = 4 \\left( 18 + 1 \\right)\n\\]\nSimplify the expression inside the parentheses:\n\\[\nx^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} = 4 \\times 19\n\\]\nCalculate the product:\n\\[\nx^3 - \\frac{1}{x^3} = 76\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{76}\n\\]",
